1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,621
This little wooden bird is about two thousand two hundred years old.

2
00:00:04,621 --> 00:00:06,702
It fits in the palm of your hand.

3
00:00:06,702 --> 00:00:14,333
And some people say it is not a bird at all, but a model aeroplane, carved two thousand years before the Wright brothers.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,341
A glider, built by ancient Egyptians.

5
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:20,433
Or copied from something they saw in the sky.

6
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:22,699
You have probably seen the claim.

7
00:00:23,166 --> 00:00:26,307
So tonight we do something no museum would allow.

8
00:00:26,307 --> 00:00:31,500
We treat it like a new aircraft, and put it through a full flight-test programme.

9
00:00:31,966 --> 00:00:33,535
Pre-flight inspection.

10
00:00:33,535 --> 00:00:34,105
Logbook.

11
00:00:34,105 --> 00:00:34,747
Airframe.

12
00:00:34,747 --> 00:00:39,667
Three flight tests: by hand, in a wind tunnel, and inside a computer.

13
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:42,733
Then we ask what it was really built to do.

14
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:44,958
Every claim gets a score.

15
00:00:44,958 --> 00:00:46,505
At the end, a verdict.

16
00:00:46,505 --> 00:00:48,756
Cleared for flight, or grounded.

17
00:00:48,756 --> 00:00:49,600
Let's begin.

18
00:00:54,066 --> 00:00:55,547
Test card, item one.

19
00:00:55,547 --> 00:00:56,362
Pre-flight.

20
00:00:56,362 --> 00:01:03,766
The object sits in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo, under special register number six three four seven.

21
00:01:04,233 --> 00:01:10,065
It is fourteen point two centimetres long, with a wingspan of eighteen point three.

22
00:01:10,065 --> 00:01:11,892
Carved from sycamore wood.

23
00:01:11,892 --> 00:01:16,599
Weight, thirty-nine grams, according to the man who made it famous.

24
00:01:17,066 --> 00:01:18,792
Already, a warning light.

25
00:01:18,792 --> 00:01:23,349
Popular accounts give its weight as fourteen grams, or thirty-one.

26
00:01:23,349 --> 00:01:27,700
For an aircraft, weight is everything, and nobody agrees on it.

27
00:01:28,166 --> 00:01:38,920
It was found in eighteen ninety-eight, at Saqqara, the vast burial ground of ancient Memphis, in a tomb usually named as the tomb of Pa-di-Imen.

28
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:43,700
It is dated to around two hundred B C, the age of the Ptolemies.

29
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:46,055
But the paperwork is thin.

30
00:01:46,055 --> 00:01:52,809
No excavation report has been published that shows exactly which tomb, or what lay beside it.

31
00:01:52,809 --> 00:01:54,407
Even the year wobbles.

32
00:01:54,407 --> 00:01:57,966
One scientific abstract says eighteen ninety-one.

33
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:04,626
So we have a real ancient object, a fuzzy birth certificate, and one strange shape.

34
00:02:04,626 --> 00:02:05,894
Now, the logbook.

35
00:02:05,894 --> 00:02:08,133
How did a bird become a plane?

36
00:02:08,599 --> 00:02:09,302
Item two.

37
00:02:09,302 --> 00:02:10,238
The logbook.

38
00:02:10,238 --> 00:02:11,798
Nineteen sixty-nine.

39
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:19,599
An Egyptian doctor, Khalil Messiha, is looking at a case of ancient bird models in the Cairo museum.

40
00:02:20,066 --> 00:02:25,785
Messiha taught anatomy for artists, and he had built model aeroplanes since childhood.

41
00:02:25,785 --> 00:02:28,046
He belonged to model flying clubs.

42
00:02:28,046 --> 00:02:33,166
So when he looked at this one bird, he saw something the others did not show.

43
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:34,233
No legs.

44
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:35,733
No painted feathers.

45
00:02:35,733 --> 00:02:36,858
Straight wings.

46
00:02:36,858 --> 00:02:41,058
And a tail that stands upright, like the fin of a plane.

47
00:02:41,058 --> 00:02:44,433
Real birds, he argued, hold their tails flat.

48
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:49,687
His brother Guirguis, a flight engineer, built a copy in balsa wood.

49
00:02:49,687 --> 00:02:58,699
Messiha added a horizontal tailplane, which he supposed had been lost, and wrote that it sailed a few yards when thrown by hand.

50
00:02:59,166 --> 00:03:00,886
His conclusion was bold.

51
00:03:00,886 --> 00:03:09,199
This was a scale model of a full-size monoplane, and somewhere in Saqqara, the real aircraft might still be waiting.

52
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:15,340
In nineteen seventy-two the idea went public, in an Egyptian travel magazine.

53
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:23,666
Reports say the Cairo museum even staged a small exhibition of ancient model aircraft, with the bird as its star.

54
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:26,328
Then the story started to grow.

55
00:03:26,328 --> 00:03:31,709
A journalist added a groove under the tail, where a tailplane would slot in.

56
00:03:31,709 --> 00:03:35,533
Messiha himself had described no groove, and no holes.

57
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:47,150
A magazine wrote that a separate slotted piece fits onto the tail, that it soars with a slight jerk of the hand, and that a full-size version could carry heavy loads.

58
00:03:47,150 --> 00:03:49,366
A newspaper gave it landing gear.

59
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:57,682
Online, it now carries hieroglyphs saying the gift of Amun, and three papyri nearby that say, I want to fly.

60
00:03:57,682 --> 00:03:59,863
None of that is in any record.

61
00:03:59,863 --> 00:04:03,933
Gift of Amun is simply what the tomb owner's name means.

62
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:10,817
One site even says Erich von Däniken used the bird in his nineteen sixty-eight bestseller.

63
00:04:10,817 --> 00:04:14,952
That book came out a year before anyone called it a plane.

64
00:04:14,952 --> 00:04:20,299
In the ancient astronaut world, it sits beside the golden jets of Colombia.

65
00:04:20,766 --> 00:04:26,215
So the logbook shows one honest observation, and a pile of later upgrades.

66
00:04:26,215 --> 00:04:28,866
Time to inspect the airframe itself.

67
00:04:29,333 --> 00:04:30,128
Item three.

68
00:04:30,128 --> 00:04:31,575
Airframe inspection.

69
00:04:31,575 --> 00:04:37,866
Let's look at it the way an aircraft engineer would, and the way an Egyptologist would.

70
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:39,384
First, the head.

71
00:04:39,384 --> 00:04:41,485
It has a beak and a painted eye.

72
00:04:41,485 --> 00:04:47,001
There is paint on the top of the tail, and two faint reddish lines around the belly.

73
00:04:47,001 --> 00:04:51,466
Underneath, the body curves from head to tail like a bird in flight.

74
00:04:51,933 --> 00:04:53,207
Second, the wings.

75
00:04:53,207 --> 00:04:56,109
From the front, the tips droop downwards.

76
00:04:56,109 --> 00:04:58,162
Engineers call that anhedral.

77
00:04:58,162 --> 00:05:03,399
On a small glider, it makes things worse: it tips the model into a spiral.

78
00:05:03,866 --> 00:05:05,464
Third, the wing joint.

79
00:05:05,464 --> 00:05:10,765
The wing is held on with iron nails, sits off-centre and slightly skewed.

80
00:05:10,765 --> 00:05:16,066
A glider expert concluded it had probably been remounted in modern times.

81
00:05:16,533 --> 00:05:27,588
That expert is Martin Gregorie, who has designed and flown free-flight competition gliders for more than thirty years, and placed fifth at a world championship.

82
00:05:27,588 --> 00:05:30,766
He lists three things any stable glider needs.

83
00:05:31,233 --> 00:05:31,510
One.

84
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:37,184
The balance point must sit roughly a quarter to sixty percent back along the wing.

85
00:05:37,184 --> 00:05:41,267
The Saqqara bird balances at, or behind, the trailing edge.

86
00:05:41,267 --> 00:05:43,966
And no weight was ever put in its head.

87
00:05:44,433 --> 00:05:44,705
Two.

88
00:05:44,705 --> 00:05:49,335
A horizontal tailplane, about a fifth to a quarter of the wing area.

89
00:05:49,335 --> 00:05:54,033
The bird has none, and there is no trace of where one was ever fixed.

90
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:54,951
Three.

91
00:05:54,951 --> 00:05:59,089
Wing tips raised, not lowered, to stop it rolling over.

92
00:05:59,089 --> 00:06:01,045
The bird has the opposite.

93
00:06:01,045 --> 00:06:02,399
Zero out of three.

94
00:06:02,866 --> 00:06:05,723
On paper, the airframe fails inspection.

95
00:06:05,723 --> 00:06:07,438
But paper is not flight.

96
00:06:07,438 --> 00:06:10,152
So we do what the believers asked for.

97
00:06:10,152 --> 00:06:10,866
We fly it.

98
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:12,477
Flight test one.

99
00:06:12,477 --> 00:06:13,336
Hand launch.

100
00:06:13,336 --> 00:06:25,566
In two thousand two, Gregorie built balsa replicas from careful drawings, with two wings, and two tails: one to scale, and one large tailplane, a quarter of the wing area.

101
00:06:26,033 --> 00:06:29,530
With no tail, or the scale tail, it would not fly.

102
00:06:29,530 --> 00:06:31,698
Launched slowly, it pitched up.

103
00:06:31,698 --> 00:06:33,866
Launched fast, it pitched down.

104
00:06:33,866 --> 00:06:34,985
Then it tumbled.

105
00:06:34,985 --> 00:06:37,433
Weight in the nose changed nothing.

106
00:06:37,899 --> 00:06:42,217
But mounted on a pin under the wing, it did one job beautifully.

107
00:06:42,217 --> 00:06:45,522
It turned straight into the wind and held steady.

108
00:06:45,522 --> 00:06:46,466
Remember that.

109
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:51,369
With the big tailplane fitted, it finally glided, fast and steep.

110
00:06:51,369 --> 00:06:55,190
It could not be launched with a slight jerk of the hand.

111
00:06:55,190 --> 00:06:57,033
It needed a full-arm throw.

112
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:02,294
Best result: three metres forward for a one point three metre drop.

113
00:07:02,294 --> 00:07:04,727
A glide of one to two point three.

114
00:07:04,727 --> 00:07:08,233
A modern sailplane does better than one to forty.

115
00:07:08,699 --> 00:07:15,327
Weighted up to match the real wood, he said it flew more like a brick with fins than an aircraft.

116
00:07:15,327 --> 00:07:17,172
His verdict: it never flew.

117
00:07:17,172 --> 00:07:19,699
More likely a toy, or a weather vane.

118
00:07:20,166 --> 00:07:22,025
But believers had an answer.

119
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:23,618
Hand launches are crude.

120
00:07:23,618 --> 00:07:27,866
Put it in a wind tunnel, and the numbers tell a different story.

121
00:07:28,333 --> 00:07:29,503
Flight test two.

122
00:07:29,503 --> 00:07:30,674
The wind tunnel.

123
00:07:30,674 --> 00:07:42,600
In two thousand six, a television documentary had an aerodynamics specialist, Simon Sanderson, build a larger replica and put it in the airflow, with no tailplane.

124
00:07:43,066 --> 00:07:47,770
The headline result: it produced four times its own weight in lift.

125
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:51,000
Clips of that moment still circulate as proof.

126
00:07:51,466 --> 00:07:53,237
But read the small print.

127
00:07:53,237 --> 00:07:58,479
The model was held in place by cables, and the cables did the stabilising.

128
00:07:58,479 --> 00:07:59,824
Lift is not flight.

129
00:07:59,824 --> 00:08:01,524
A flat plank makes lift.

130
00:08:01,524 --> 00:08:05,066
Keeping it pointed the right way is the hard part.

131
00:08:05,533 --> 00:08:13,349
Then the programme took the data to a flight simulator at Liverpool University, and added a stabilising tailplane.

132
00:08:13,349 --> 00:08:17,119
With the tailplane, the simulated bird flew quite well.

133
00:08:17,119 --> 00:08:21,233
The part that made it fly was the part that was never found.

134
00:08:21,699 --> 00:08:28,305
In two thousand eleven, for William Shatner's TV series Weird or What, Gregorie went further.

135
00:08:28,305 --> 00:08:35,833
New replicas, a mechanical launcher for repeatable throws, careful measurements, and the tailplane fitted.

136
00:08:36,299 --> 00:08:41,342
The glide ratios came in between about two point two and two point eight.

137
00:08:41,342 --> 00:08:46,177
Gregorie put it plainly: that is about as good as a man in a wingsuit.

138
00:08:46,177 --> 00:08:48,733
Even the space shuttle glides better.

139
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:54,380
With the drooping scale wing, it rolled forty degrees within two metres.

140
00:08:54,380 --> 00:08:57,978
And without the tailplane, it tumbled, every time.

141
00:08:57,978 --> 00:08:59,633
Two tests, one pattern.

142
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:01,397
Flight test three.

143
00:09:01,397 --> 00:09:02,622
The digital twin.

144
00:09:02,622 --> 00:09:04,640
Replicas can be built wrong.

145
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:13,000
So in twenty twenty-three, two engineers from the Institute of Aerospace Technology in Bremen tested the real shape.

146
00:09:13,466 --> 00:09:28,066
Michel Zierow and Leon Lesemann used a 3D photo scan of the artefact, made with staff of the Egyptian Museum, rebuilt it in seventy-four slices, and ran a full computer airflow simulation.

147
00:09:28,533 --> 00:09:32,705
Lift peaked at a low angle and then the flow broke away.

148
00:09:32,705 --> 00:09:36,580
Drag was two to five times that of a light aircraft.

149
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:39,933
Best lift to drag ratio: two point eight six.

150
00:09:40,399 --> 00:09:46,974
To glide at all, it would need about seventeen metres per second, roughly sixty kilometres an hour.

151
00:09:46,974 --> 00:09:50,759
Too fast, the authors wrote, for a stable launch by hand.

152
00:09:50,759 --> 00:09:57,333
Dropped from two metres, its best flight is five and a half metres, lasting one and a half seconds.

153
00:09:57,799 --> 00:10:06,378
Its centre of gravity sits fifty-four millimetres behind the nose, near the trailing edge, behind the point that would keep it stable.

154
00:10:06,378 --> 00:10:07,466
So it pitches up.

155
00:10:07,933 --> 00:10:11,600
And the lift is lopsided, stronger on the left wing.

156
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:15,689
So it rolls right, banks, pitches up, stalls, and crashes.

157
00:10:15,689 --> 00:10:18,933
In their words, it cannot fly a straight path.

158
00:10:19,399 --> 00:10:22,293
Three tests, three methods, one result.

159
00:10:22,293 --> 00:10:25,260
As it is, the Saqqara bird does not fly.

160
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:27,931
But the engineers ended with a hint.

161
00:10:27,931 --> 00:10:30,305
Look at it as a cultural object.

162
00:10:30,305 --> 00:10:33,866
And that vertical fin might suit a weather vane.

163
00:10:34,333 --> 00:10:35,136
Item seven.

164
00:10:35,136 --> 00:10:36,232
Alternate role.

165
00:10:36,232 --> 00:10:38,423
If not an aircraft, then what?

166
00:10:38,423 --> 00:10:40,468
Start with the temple walls.

167
00:10:40,468 --> 00:10:48,866
At Karnak, in the Temple of Khonsu, reliefs of sacred festival boats show small bird figures on top of their masts.

168
00:10:49,333 --> 00:10:54,412
A bird on a pivot, with an upright fin, turns its beak into the wind.

169
00:10:54,412 --> 00:10:57,650
That is exactly what Gregorie's replica did.

170
00:10:57,650 --> 00:11:03,833
A wind vane, on a boat, a shrine or a roof, explains the strangest feature, the fin.

171
00:11:04,299 --> 00:11:05,559
Or a sacred bird.

172
00:11:05,559 --> 00:11:10,301
The falcon was the form of Horus and of the sun god Ra-Horakhty.

173
00:11:10,301 --> 00:11:18,600
Egyptian bird figures were made for coffins, shrines and offerings, most with folded wings and painted feathers.

174
00:11:19,066 --> 00:11:19,723
Or a toy.

175
00:11:19,723 --> 00:11:23,009
Wikipedia describes it as mounted on a stick.

176
00:11:23,009 --> 00:11:29,362
A child running with a wooden bird held high would make the same shape against the sky.

177
00:11:29,362 --> 00:11:34,766
Toy, vane, falcon, masthead: none of these is proven, but all of them fit.

178
00:11:35,233 --> 00:11:36,854
And the era matters.

179
00:11:36,854 --> 00:11:44,797
Two hundred B C is the Ptolemaic age, a time of real invention: gears, screws, valves, Archimedes.

180
00:11:44,797 --> 00:11:46,824
Clever people lived then.

181
00:11:46,824 --> 00:11:50,066
But they left texts and machines behind.

182
00:11:50,533 --> 00:11:52,984
For aviation, there is nothing.

183
00:11:52,984 --> 00:11:54,566
No full-size glider.

184
00:11:54,566 --> 00:11:55,515
No workshop.

185
00:11:55,515 --> 00:11:56,938
No text on flight.

186
00:11:56,938 --> 00:11:59,231
No image of a flying machine.

187
00:11:59,231 --> 00:12:01,366
Just one small wooden bird.

188
00:12:01,833 --> 00:12:06,192
And the alien version, that it copies a craft visitors flew?

189
00:12:06,192 --> 00:12:11,206
It has even less: no craft, no visitors, and a model that cannot fly.

190
00:12:11,206 --> 00:12:14,766
It depends on reading a falcon's fin as a rudder.

191
00:12:15,233 --> 00:12:15,828
Debrief.

192
00:12:15,828 --> 00:12:18,133
Time to score the Saqqara bird.

193
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:22,944
A real ancient carving, about two hundred B C: evidence.

194
00:12:22,944 --> 00:12:27,210
Shaped like a bird, with beak, eye and paint: evidence.

195
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:32,098
An upright fin unlike other bird figures: real, and still open.

196
00:12:32,098 --> 00:12:35,666
A vane, a masthead, a toy or a falcon: likely.

197
00:12:36,133 --> 00:12:40,088
A missing tailplane, or a groove for one: no trace.

198
00:12:40,088 --> 00:12:43,965
Flies as it is: debunked, by three separate tests.

199
00:12:43,965 --> 00:12:47,533
Alien or lost aviation knowledge: no evidence.

200
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:48,948
The verdict.

201
00:12:48,948 --> 00:12:49,659
Grounded.

202
00:12:49,659 --> 00:12:57,485
The Saqqara bird is a real and lovely carving of a bird, probably a vane, a sacred figure or a toy.

203
00:12:57,485 --> 00:13:00,409
It is not a glider, and it never was.

204
00:13:00,409 --> 00:13:03,966
But its fin is still a small, honest mystery.

205
00:13:04,433 --> 00:13:10,235
Somewhere, twenty-two centuries ago, a carver made a bird with a tail that stands up.

206
00:13:10,235 --> 00:13:12,693
Maybe because it turned in the wind.

207
00:13:12,693 --> 00:13:15,833
That idea is wonderful enough, without aliens.

208
00:13:16,299 --> 00:13:21,687
Next case: one man, more than a thousand tons of coral rock, and no witnesses.

209
00:13:21,687 --> 00:13:23,000
How did he move it?

210
00:13:23,466 --> 00:13:25,589
What do you think the fin was for?

211
00:13:25,589 --> 00:13:30,833
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
